A||||4||jimmybpei||rep;acing poly bushings||||1||
Z||000000||jimmybpei||12-26-2001||12:08 PM||||What is the easiest way to get the old rubber bushings out and are there any tricks to putting the poly bushings in.<P>Thanks for the help<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jimmybpeisig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||12-26-2001||03:42 PM||||The easiest way is to burn them out.  I use a regular propane torch.....just heat the outer sleeve up until it starts smoking/melting the rubber then light one side of the bushing on fire (it'll probably do this by itself).   Keep heating the sleeve with the torch and make sure you point it away from you!<P>The gases let off by the burning rubber will force the bushing out of the sleeve.....it'll actually pop out of teh sleeve by itself.<P>If you got the sleeve hot enough there should be no (or very little) residual rubber left in the sleeve.  What I do is while it is still hot take a wet  or damp washcloth (one that you will not need to use again) and I wipe the inner sleeves.  You need to be careful because the metal is VERY hot.  Wipe out any excess rubber and let cool.  <P>Once all the bushings are burned out and the control arms are cool you can install the poly bushings.  Use plenty of the grease that is supplied with the bushings (I highly recommend buying extra).  Apply liberally inside the metal sleeve, on the bushing itself and on the small metal inner sleeve (where the bolt goes through).  This grease is very thick and sticky, so I would suggest buying some rubber gloves (like doctors examination gloves).<P>Theer is no special skill required to actually install the poly......just push them in place.  You may need to hit them with a hammer to get them fully seated, but that's it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||12-26-2001||04:08 PM||||There's another way to do it that doesn't involved any hot melted rubber. Use a 1/4 inch drill bit and drill around the inner sleve until you can tap it out with a hammer and screwdriver. Once the sleve is out you can drill four holes, one on each axis, ie..N, S, E, W. Then the rubber will pry out with a screwdriver. It took me about 3-5 minutes per bushing. You don't have to wait for the metal to cool.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||12-27-2001||01:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BAM-BAM:<BR><B>There's another way to do it that doesn't involved any hot melted rubber. Use a 1/4 inch drill bit and drill around the inner sleve until you can tap it out with a hammer and screwdriver. Once the sleve is out you can drill four holes, one on each axis, ie..N, S, E, W. Then the rubber will pry out with a screwdriver. It took me about 3-5 minutes per bushing. You don't have to wait for the metal to cool.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was just going to suggest that! Beat me to it. Good thing you're on the ball, B2. I guess all that money Cliff pays you is doing some good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric_PA||12-27-2001||06:31 AM||||I have a real easy way for you. Get yourself a set of hole saws and first get the diameter that is a bit larger than the inner sleeve and cut as far as you can. Now once that is done, pry out the center part and find a larger hole saw that is slightly smaller than the outer part and cut through again. It should pry out really easy. This has worked very well for me, but make sure you use a high quality drill, or it's going to burn up your drill.<P>Eric||||reg||10
